<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#rye">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rye</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rye is a hardy cereal grain that grows well in cool climates and poorer soils, long a staple of northern and eastern European agriculture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> exported large quantities of rye and wheat through the Baltic port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. According to the Commonwealth profile, this grain trade enriched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> nobility and encouraged the tightening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rye.jpg" alt="Rye" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LSDSL · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ear_of_rye.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rye (Secale cereale) belongs to the wheat tribe, Triticeae, which also includes wheat and barley, and is one of several cereals that grow wild in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Evidence from the Epipalaeolithic site of Tell Abu Hureyra in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> suggests that rye was among the first cereal crops to be systematically cultivated, around 13,000 years ago, but critics point to inconsistencies in the radiocarbon dates. Domesticated rye occurs in small quantities at a number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> but is otherwise absent from the archaeological record until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, around 1800-1500 BCE.</p><p>It probably reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as a minor admixture in wheat and was only later cultivated in its own right. Remains have been found in Roman contexts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland. Pliny the Elder, writing of a grain that may have been rye, called it a very poor food that only served to avert starvation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Rye is a tall grass, annual or biennial, reaching 1 to 3 metres, with seeds carried in a curved spike. It is much more tolerant of cold and poor soil than other cereals, grows better than any other in heavy clay and light sandy soil, and survives snow cover that would kill winter wheat. It has therefore been especially valuable where the soil is sandy or peaty. Winter rye, sown in autumn, grows again in spring early enough to provide grazing.</p><p>Rye is highly susceptible to the ergot fungus. Eating infected rye causes ergotism, with convulsions, miscarriage, necrosis of the digits, hallucinations and death, and damp northern countries that depended on rye were historically subject to periodic epidemics. Modern grain cleaning has practically eliminated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> rye has been widely cultivated in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, where it is the main bread cereal in most areas east of the France-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> border and north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; in Southern Europe it was grown on marginal lands. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> it was a staple food in the Middle Ages, and rye crispbread remains popular there. Rye bread, including pumpernickel, is widely eaten in Northern and Eastern Europe. The grain also makes rye beer and rye whiskey, and the drink kvass is fermented from rye bread or flour and malt.</p><p>Rye straw is used as livestock bedding, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> rye flour is boiled with red iron oxide to make the traditional Falun red house paint. In European folklore the Roggenwolf, or rye wolf, is a wolf-shaped field spirit that steals children, and the last heads of grain were often left as a sacrifice to such spirits. The classical scholar Carl A. P. Ruck links these figures with ergot, whose reddish infected grains were known as wolf teeth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most people lived in small hamlets of timber longhouses and sunken-floored huts, often close to abandoned Roman villas whose stone buildings they rarely reused, growing barley, wheat and <b>rye</b> and raising cattle and pigs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Families farmed small terraced fields of <b>rye</b>, barley and later potatoes, cut hay for the winter and, above all, herded sheep and cattle on high summer pastures, moving flocks seasonally to the lowlands of Catalonia and southern France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture made up 70% of the national income, and most farms, the folwarks, were large noble estates worked by landless peasants; exports of wheat and <b>rye</b> stabilized the finances, and Great Britain was an important buyer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The grand duchy was a land of forest, lakes and rivers where most people farmed <b>rye</b>, kept livestock and paid dues in kind to their lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Peasants grew <b>rye</b>, oats and barley on the poor soils of the forest zone, supplementing farming with fishing, hunting, beekeeping and the trapping of furs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The population of Rus' was overwhelmingly East Slavic and rural, cultivating <b>rye</b>, wheat, barley and millet in clearings of the forest zone and on the richer soils of the forest-steppe, with Finnic and Baltic peoples in the north and west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
